\section{Hybrid Stability Across Live Reconfiguration}
\label{sec:hybrid-live-stability}

The normal-Live results above deliberately exclude hard attitude rewrites from
one finite-step lift.  The deployed wrapper nevertheless contains two such
reconfiguration events: the one-time second-stage magnetic refinement and the
Live tilt re-lock.  This section treats those events as jump maps and composes
them with Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-live-capture}.  The purpose is not to pretend
that a hard reset is an ordinary Kalman sample, but to state exactly what must
hold on each side of the jump for the regional argument to continue.

Let the hybrid estimator state be $(\vct e,\pi)$, where $\vct e$ is the local
MEKF error and $\pi$ contains the covariance, state-enable flags, magnetic
reference and applied hard-iron offset, and the staged OU/$R_S$ schedule.
Normal Live samples evolve through the exact map
$\mathcal F_{k,m}$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-exact-lift}.  A hard event instead
applies
\begin{equation}
 (\vct e^+,\pi^+)=\mathcal R_j(\vct e^-,\pi^-;\mu_j),
 \qquad j\in\{M,T\},
 \label{eq:hybrid-jump-map}
\end{equation}
where $M$ denotes magnetic refinement, $T$ denotes tilt re-lock, and $\mu_j$
collects the bounded measurements/reference quantities admitted by that jump.
No normal-Live contraction claim is used across Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-jump-map}.

\subsection{One-time magnetic-reference refinement}
\label{sec:hybrid-mag-jump}

The source path \texttt{maybeRefineMagReference\_()} replaces the world magnetic
reference, optionally rewrites nominal yaw to the independently acquired
absolute yaw, leaves roll/pitch and every additive navigation state numerically
unchanged, and releases the external accelerometer-bias hold.  When a finite
refinement yaw $\psi_M$ is available,
\begin{equation}
 q^+_{bw}=R_z(\psi_M)\,\mathcal Q_{\rm tilt}(q^-_{bw}),
 \label{eq:hybrid-mag-attitude-map}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal Q_{\rm tilt}$ is the exact yaw-removal helper used by the source;
otherwise $q^+_{bw}=q^-_{bw}$.  Hence the map does not replace tilt.  At the
source-variable level,
\begin{equation}
 (b_g,v,p,S,a_w,b_a)^+=(b_g,v,p,S,a_w,b_a)^-.
 \label{eq:hybrid-mag-additive-map}
\end{equation}
The call to \texttt{set\_quaternion\_boat()} does not overwrite covariance.
Releasing the bias hold can, if the normal bias gate has already unlocked,
re-enable accelerometer-bias updates and raise $P_{b_ab_a}$ to its configured
initialization floor.  Denote that bounded covariance/flag operation by
\begin{equation}
 \pi^+=\mathcal P_M(\pi^-,B_M,\psi_M).
 \label{eq:hybrid-mag-mode-map}
\end{equation}
Thus the event is not a generic covariance reset.

The horizontal-gauge objective of Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-heading-gauge} is essential
here.  Let $b_{\psi,\mathrm{ref}}^-$ and
$b_{\psi,\mathrm{ref}}^+$ denote the old and new residual heading-reference
offsets and define
\begin{equation}
 \Delta\psi_M
 :=b_{\psi,\mathrm{ref}}^+-b_{\psi,\mathrm{ref}}^-,
 \qquad
 \mat Q_M:=R_z(\Delta\psi_M).
 \label{eq:hybrid-mag-gauge-change}
\end{equation}
The refinement changes the adopted \emph{horizontal frame}, not merely the
attitude reference.  Therefore the gauge-referenced physical world vectors of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-gauge-world-vectors} satisfy
\begin{equation}
 x_g^+=\mat Q_M x_g^- ,
 \qquad x\in\{v,p,S,a_w\}.
 \label{eq:hybrid-mag-world-reference-map}
\end{equation}
The source does not rotate the stored nominal additive states.  Consequently,
for $e_x:=\widehat x-x_g$,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 e_x^+=e_x^-+(\mat I-\mat Q_M)x_g^- ,
 \qquad x\in\{v,p,S,a_w\}.}
 \label{eq:hybrid-mag-world-error-jump}
\end{equation}
This is a coordinate-reference jump, not physical translational instability,
but it must be included in a gauge-referenced regional certificate.

Assume the admitted one-time refinement family satisfies
\begin{equation}
 |\Delta\psi_M|\le\overline\Delta_M<\pi,
 \qquad
 |\delta\psi_{\rm int}^+|\le\overline\epsilon_M,
 \label{eq:hybrid-mag-heading-residual}
\end{equation}
and the physical world-vector bounds
\begin{equation}
 \|v_g^-\|\le\overline V_M,
 \quad \|p_g^-\|\le\overline P_M,
 \quad \|S_g^-\|\le\overline S_M,
 \quad \|a_{w,g}^-\|\le\overline A_M.
 \label{eq:hybrid-mag-world-bounds}
\end{equation}
The first heading bound limits the frame change; the second bounds estimator
heading relative to the new gauge.  Non-yaw magnetic-model error remains in the
normal bounded model-disturbance family.

Since
$\|\mat I-R_z(\alpha)\|_2=2|\sin(\alpha/2)|$,
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-mag-world-error-jump} gives
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|e_x^+\|
 \le\|e_x^-\|+2s_M\overline X_M,
 \qquad s_M:=\sin(\overline\Delta_M/2),}
 \label{eq:hybrid-mag-world-error-bound}
\end{equation}
where $(\overline X_M,x)$ is one of
$(\overline V_M,v)$, $(\overline P_M,p)$,
$(\overline S_M,S)$, or $(\overline A_M,a_w)$.

For any structured pre-jump radius $\vct a$, define the exact magnetic-jump
envelope
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 [\mathcal J_M(\vct a)]_i
 :=\sup\left\{
 r_i\!\left(\mathcal R_M(\vct e,\pi;\mu_M)\right):
 \vct e\in\mathcal C(\vct a),\ \pi\in\Pi_M,\ \mu_M\in\mathcal M_M
 \right\},}
 \quad i\in\{\xi,\ell\},
 \label{eq:hybrid-mag-envelope}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal M_M$ contains the accepted reference/yaw and physical-state
family satisfying Eqs.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-mag-heading-residual}--
\eqref{eq:hybrid-mag-world-bounds}.  The complete source/reference map is
continuous on this compact family, so $\mathcal J_M$ is finite and monotone in
$\vct a$.

For the fixed dimensionless block norm of
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:iss-block-xi}--\eqref{eq:iss-block-ell}, conservative witnesses
for the coordinate-reference part are
\begin{align}
 \|z_\ell^+\|
 &\le \|z_\ell^-\|
 +2s_M\left[
   (\overline V_M/s_v)^2+(\overline P_M/s_p)^2
 \right]^{1/2},
 \label{eq:hybrid-mag-fixed-kinematic-bound}\\
 \|z_\xi^+\|
 &\le \|z_\xi^-\|
 +\left[
   (\overline\epsilon_M/s_\theta)^2
  +(2s_M\overline S_M/s_S)^2
  +(2s_M\overline A_M/s_{aw})^2
 \right]^{1/2}.
 \label{eq:hybrid-mag-fixed-sensitive-bound}
\end{align}
These bounds deliberately do not credit cancellation or removal of the old
heading residual, so the exact envelope Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-mag-envelope} is
the preferred certificate object.

The refinement is one-shot by source construction:
\texttt{mag\_refine\_done\_} blocks every subsequent call after the first
successful replacement.  Continuous hard-iron correction after that event is
different.  It writes no attitude state or covariance; it slews the applied
body-frame offset and magnetic model and therefore belongs to the bounded
normal-Live mode family $\pi_k$, not to $\mathcal R_M$.

\subsection{Live tilt re-lock}
\label{sec:hybrid-tilt-jump}

When the estimated boat tilt exceeds $70^\circ$ for the configured hold and the
cooldown has expired, Live mode calls
\texttt{initialize\_from\_acc\_preserve\_yaw()}.  The exact nominal-state map
is
\begin{equation}
 q^+_{bw}=R_z(\psi^-_{\rm E})Q_y(\vartheta_a)Q_x(\varphi_a),
 \label{eq:hybrid-tilt-attitude-map}
\end{equation}
where $\psi^-_{\rm E}$ is the ZYX Euler yaw extracted from the pre-jump
attitude and $(\varphi_a,\vartheta_a)$ are the accelerometer-only roll/pitch.
All additive nominal states are unchanged,
\begin{equation}
 (b_g,v,p,S,a_w,b_a)^+=(b_g,v,p,S,a_w,b_a)^-.
 \label{eq:hybrid-tilt-additive-map}
\end{equation}
The attitude covariance is reseeded with the accelerometer-only tilt/yaw
covariance, attitude--gyro-bias, attitude--linear-state, and
attitude--accelerometer-bias cross-covariances are zeroed, and the remaining
covariance blocks are retained.  Write this bounded source operation as
\begin{equation}
 \pi^+=\mathcal P_T(\pi^-).
 \label{eq:hybrid-tilt-mode-map}
\end{equation}

A deterministic reset guarantee requires a condition on the accelerometer
sample used by the hard map.  Let $u_d$ be the true body-frame down direction
and write
\begin{equation}
 f_b=-g u_d+\epsilon_f,
 \qquad
 \|\epsilon_f\|\le\overline f_T<g.
 \label{eq:hybrid-tilt-force-bound}
\end{equation}
The perturbation includes physical nongravitational acceleration, residual
accelerometer bias, bounded deterministic sensor error, and bounded
lever-arm/model defect.  The closed ball of radius $\overline f_T$ about
$-g u_d$ does not contain the origin, and elementary tangent geometry gives
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|\delta\theta_\perp^+\|
 \le \overline\theta_T
 :=\sin^{-1}(\overline f_T/g)<\frac{\pi}{2}.}
 \label{eq:hybrid-tilt-reset-bound}
\end{equation}
The current $70^\circ$/hold trigger does not itself impose
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-force-bound}; that measurement-quality condition is
therefore explicit rather than inferred from the trigger.

The source preserves the ZYX Euler yaw coordinate, not the local rotation-vector
yaw component exactly.  Define the pre/post Euler-yaw error relative to the
unchanged adopted horizontal gauge by
\begin{equation}
 \epsilon_{\psi,E}:=\wrap(\widehat\psi_E-\psi_{g,E}).
 \label{eq:hybrid-tilt-euler-yaw-error}
\end{equation}
On an admitted chart satisfying
\begin{equation}
 |\cos\vartheta^-|\ge c_\psi>0,
 \qquad
 |\cos\vartheta_g|\ge c_\psi>0,
 \label{eq:hybrid-tilt-yaw-chart}
\end{equation}
the source map gives the exact coordinate identity
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \epsilon_{\psi,E}^+=\epsilon_{\psi,E}^-.}
 \label{eq:hybrid-tilt-yaw-preserved}
\end{equation}
On any compact subset of this nonsingular Euler chart, the smooth coordinate
map from Euler attitude difference to the local error vector is Lipschitz.
Hence finite constants $L_{\psi,T},c_{\psi,T}$ exist such that
\begin{equation}
 |\delta\psi_{\rm int}^+|
 \le L_{\psi,T}|\epsilon_{\psi,E}^-|
    +c_{\psi,T}\|\delta\theta_\perp^+\|.
 \label{eq:hybrid-tilt-local-yaw-bound}
\end{equation}
The exact jump envelope below evaluates the source map directly; this inequality
is only an analytical witness explaining why preserving Euler yaw does not
create an unbounded local heading error away from the Euler singularity.  A
geometric yaw-preserving source map would be needed to remove the chart
condition entirely.

Let $\mathcal G_T\subset\mathcal C(\vct R_D)$ be the declared re-lock guard
family satisfying Eqs.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-force-bound} and
\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-yaw-chart}.  Define
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 [\mathcal J_T(\mathcal G_T)]_i
 :=\sup\left\{
 r_i\!\left(\mathcal R_T(\vct e,\pi;\mu_T)\right):
 (\vct e,\pi,\mu_T)\in\mathcal G_T
 \right\},}
 \quad i\in\{\xi,\ell\}.
 \label{eq:hybrid-tilt-envelope}
\end{equation}
This is the exact post-reset envelope.  Because the adopted horizontal gauge is
unchanged, the kinematic gauge reference does not jump.  The sensitive radius
replaces pre-jump tilt by the bounded accelerometer-based tilt, maps the
preserved Euler-yaw error into the local attitude chart, retains the additive
bias/$S$/$a_w$ states, and changes covariance mode according to
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-mode-map}.

The wrapper sets a $3\,$s cooldown after every Live tilt reset and refuses a
new reset while that timer is positive.  Therefore reset times satisfy
\begin{equation}
 t^T_{j+1}-t^T_j\ge T_{\rm cool}=3\ \mathrm{s},
 \label{eq:hybrid-tilt-dwell}
\end{equation}
which rules out Zeno accumulation of tilt jumps.  The $0.35\,$s trigger hold
does not increase this guaranteed inter-reset spacing, because it can accrue
while the cooldown timer is running.

\subsection{Composition with finite-step regional capture}
\label{sec:hybrid-composition}

The useful jump conditions are not contraction conditions.  A safety reset or
reference-frame change may make the structured error larger.  It is enough that
each admissible jump returns the estimator to the already-certified outer
capture region:
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathcal J_M(\vct R_D)\preceq\vct R_C,
 \qquad
 \mathcal J_T(\mathcal G_T)\preceq\vct R_C.}
 \label{eq:hybrid-jump-entry}
\end{equation}
For the magnetic event the first condition may be checked on a smaller known
pre-refinement family when available; $\vct R_D$ is simply the strongest
uniform statement.  Each jump mode must also leave its post-jump covariance and
measurement parameters inside the compact mode family used by subsequent
comparison envelopes.

\begin{theorem}[Hybrid regional safety and repeated recovery]
\label{thm:hybrid-live-recovery}
Assume Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-live-capture} on
$\mathcal C(\vct R_D)$, the jump-entry conditions
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-jump-entry}, and the source/guard conditions stated above.
Assume every normal-Live interval between jumps follows an admissible mode
sequence of $\Pi_{k,m}$.  Then:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=1.6em]
 \item every hybrid trajectory beginning in $\mathcal C(\vct R_C)$ remains in
       $\mathcal C(\vct R_D)$ through any finite sequence of admissible magnetic
       refinement and tilt re-lock events;
 \item if the hard-jump sequence is finite, then after its final jump the
       trajectory enters $\mathcal B$ in finite time and thereafter satisfies
       the practical-ISS estimate of Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-practical-iss};
 \item if tilt re-locks occur infinitely often, the source cooldown
       Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-dwell} prevents Zeno behavior.  On every
       inter-jump interval long enough to contain the certified outer-to-inner
       capture sequence, the trajectory re-enters $\mathcal B$ before the next
       jump.  Infinite repeated hard resets therefore support a recurrent
       capture claim, but not convergence to zero unless the resets eventually
       cease or a stronger jump/flow contraction condition is separately
       verified.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediately after either admissible hard event,
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-jump-entry} places the gauge-referenced error in
$\mathcal C(\vct R_C)$.  From there the prefix-domain condition
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-prefix-domain} keeps every ordinary Live sample inside
$\mathcal C(\vct R_D)$ until capture.  If another hard event occurs first, its
pre-jump state remains in the declared safe domain and its jump condition
returns it to $\mathcal C(\vct R_C)$.  Induction over the jump count proves
(i).  If jumps stop, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-live-capture} applies from the last
post-jump outer-set entry and proves (ii).  The source cooldown gives a positive
lower bound on successive tilt-reset times, hence no Zeno sequence.  Whenever
an inter-jump interval contains the full certified capture sequence, the same
finite-step theorem reaches $\mathcal B$ before the following jump, proving
(iii).  Without such intervals, repeated hard resets can keep injecting bounded
error indefinitely, so asymptotic convergence cannot be inferred from the
normal-Live contraction alone.
\end{proof}

For a certified outer capture time $T_C$, the simple source-level sufficient
condition for re-entry between every pair of tilt resets is
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{T_C\le T_{\rm cool}=3\ \mathrm{s}.}
 \label{eq:hybrid-cooldown-capture-condition}
\end{equation}
This condition is intentionally not asserted numerically here; it is a quantity
for the later regional verifier.  If it fails, Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid-live-recovery}
still gives hybrid safety and finite-jump recovery.  A less conservative
infinite-reset result can instead be certified directly from the actual guarded
jump/flow sequence without changing the estimator.
